{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NK8A9iueXRovioFjkMnd2X","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# WI-573 — ADJUDICATE amended-cell meaning/clarity — commit 07cbabb\n2\t\n3\tOne line per amended row. Question judged: did the amendment change the\n4\trequirement's MEANING, or only its CLARITY?\n5\t\n6\t**Scope, and why the count is two.** The material I was handed is the whole\n7\tdrift set of the live registries against `docs/archive/last_approved` (copied\n8\t2026-08-30, commit `4824c0ba`) — twenty-seven rows. This row's own generated\n9\t`## Context` names **two**: `LLR-136` and `LLR-158`. The other twenty-five are\n10\tcarried by other rows and are reproduced below as reading aid, excluded from the\n11\tcounter — the same correction WI-566's REVIEW-A finding 1 forced on its first\n12\tissue (it had re-counted seventeen SR rows WI-547 had already closed). The\n13\tgoverning `VERDICT:` line is the last line of this file and it is the only one.\n14\t\n15\t## In-scope amended rows — adjudicated here, counted (2)\n16\t\n17\t- [MEANING] LLR-136 `Detail` -> the converter's write side, its round-trip `--verify`, `write_spec_file` as the single writer, and its four refusals; nothing said about where the READ side takes its population from or about the schema constant's twin -> the same, PLUS two new binding statements: `read_specs` takes its population from `spec_paths` (the `kitlib.registry.spec_files` re-export) rather than from a second folder walk, so a tracked non-spec file under a status directory is residue to BOTH sides; and `COLUMNS` is pinned equal to its read-side twin `kitlib.registry.WI_COLUMNS` -> a design conforming to the old text could carry its own read-side folder walk and its own unpinned column list and be correct; under the new text both are defects. Two obligations were added to the row, not restated. (Verified present in the tree: `wi_convert.py:513` `spec_paths = _kitregistry.spec_files`, `wi_convert.py:632` the walk, and the pin asserted at `tests/test_wi_convert.py:648` — the added text is accurate, which is why it is blessable in principle; see the aftermath below for why it is not re-anchored.)\n18\t- [MEANING] LLR-158 `Detail` -> `split_changed_cells` as the one comparison basis: the structural id/Status exclusions, `spine_cell_class` as the shared classifier, `_APPROVED_TEXT`, the before/after pairs, and needs covered by the whole-file copy -> the same, PLUS an entire second half: ONE two-tree walk `_spine_row_sides` feeding FOUR named consumers (`staged_spine_amendments`, `staged_approval_acts`, `staged_drafted_rows`, `lane_approval_refusal`), the exempts-vs-reports invariant with the de-approval subtraction, the per-row judgement stated once in `_approval_act`, and a declared registry bound (`SPINE_CSVS` / `OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT`, pinned exhaustive and disjoint against `baseline_snapshot.SNAPSHOTTED`) -> the old text obliges one function; the new text obliges a shared walk, four readers, an invariant between two of them, and a closed registry partition. An implementation satisfying the old text (one basis function, each reader walking the tree itself) fails the new one outright.\n19\t\n20\tBoth in-scope rows are MEANING, so §A5.2's \"flip back to Approved\" arm does not\n21\tapply to either.\n22\t\n23\t### The finding that governs the aftermath (LLR-158)\n24\t\n25\t`LLR-158`'s new final clause does not describe the code the row owns. It says\n26\t*\"every reader here walks `SPINE_CSVS`, the three spine registries … the four\n27\tother registries a snapshot anchors are listed in `OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT`,\n28\tand the two lists are pinned as one exhaustive, disjoint statement against\n29\t`baseline_snapshot.SNAPSHOTTED`.\"* In the tree at this commit:\n30\t\n31\t- `acceptance_record.APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS` — **four** registries, the spine three\n32\t  PLUS `stakeholder-needs.toml` — is what `staged_approval_acts` and\n33\t  `lane_approval_refusal` walk (`acceptance_record.py:144`, docstring at :541,\n34\t  test docstring at `tests/test_acceptance_record.py:216`). Two of the four\n35\t  readers therefore do NOT walk `SPINE_CSVS`.\n36\t- `OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT` lists **three** registries, not four\n37\t  (`acceptance_record.py:165`).\n38\t- The pinned identity is `SNAPSHOTTED == APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS +\n39\t  OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT` (`acceptance_record.py:162`,\n40\t  `tests/test_acceptance_record.py:234-235`) — not `SPINE_CSVS + OUTSIDE…`.\n41\t\n42\tThis is a stale cell, not a disagreement about design: the cell was written at\n43\t`d5b3e124` (\"close round 7\") and the widening landed later at `94b77a26`\n44\t(\"REVIEW-A round 028 rework … the merge refusal extended to the\n45\tstakeholder-needs tier\"), which moved `stakeholder-needs.toml` out of\n46\t`OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT` and into the act's own set. `d5b3e124` is an ancestor\n47\tof `94b77a26`; the Detail cell was never re-read against the rework it now\n48\tmisdescribes. The row's `code_symbol` cell names `OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT` and\n49\tomits `APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS`, consistent with the same miss.\n50\t\n51\t**Consequence: this session does NOT re-anchor.** The rung is released to the\n52\tloop, so a MEANING verdict here would ordinarily be re-attested by me in a\n53\tsecond commit. `LLR-158`'s new text is not text I would bless — a design row\n54\tstating a false bound is worse than a drifted one, because the bound is the very\n55\tclaim the row exists to make checkable. Anchoring is per-registry\n56\t(`baseline_snapshot.copy_live` mirrors whole registry files), so I cannot bless\n57\t`LLR-136` without also blessing `LLR-158` out of the same file. Both stay\n58\tdrifted, and the drift keeps surfacing — visibly, not silently — in the\n59\tre-attestation section of `docs/ratify/CURRENT.md` and of the generated\n60\t`open-items.html` (`trace.reattest_model` feeding `gen_open_items.py`). That is\n61\ta LOOP-side surface, not the owner's brief: this rung is released, so 